ing 1).\ncollab/dao-jing/1789700000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-final.prompt.md:55:   PostgreSQL/S3 when they're equally in-repo.\ncollab/dao-jing/1789569973938-review-jing-cbor-design.prompt.md:35:storage, like Datomic storage. Memory, files, PostgreSQL, S3, remote services,\ncollab/dao-jing/1789569973938-review-jing-cbor-design.prompt.md:67:   concretely about memory, PostgreSQL, and S3 without requiring new backend\ncollab/dao-jing/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md:17:   **Body unchanged** — S3, S6, S7 preserved verbatim, including all three\ncollab/dao-jing/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md:36:   pinning S2, S3, S6, S7 directly through `index/datoms-from-elements`\ncollab/dao-jing/1788950986527-storage-space-transactor-phase1.glm-5.3.findings.md:40:   - S3: the transaction record's exact shape — a good flatten, plus six\ntest/dao/stream/transit_test.cljc:80:           (= \"552ee5466783e35ae00f007695e632684979d86c35487d5d9d062658f30b5994\"\ncollab/dao-jing/1789630022715-architect-jing-cbor-signoff.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:28:- **Backend independence, the central invariant: holds.** The byte-store contract is opaque `(address, bytes) → :inserted | :present` and `(address, sentinel) → bytes | sentinel`. Memory, file, remote, DHT, PostgreSQL, and S3 are interchangeable at that boundary; none needs CBOR, Clojure values, or domain knowledge. No database semantics enter storage. The numeric carriers and their portable operations are representation-level, which `dao.jing.md` explicitly permits, and they are exported by the codec namespace, not the storage wrapper.\ncollab/dao-jing/1789690000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:20:- The architecture diagram (`:38`) still lists `file` as a bare peer of `memory | PostgreSQL | S3 | remote | DHT` under one undifferentiated `backend:` line — no textual marker that `file` is categorically different from the others there.\ncollab/dao-jing/1789690000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:22:- The correction's own dichotomy — \"pluggable, third-party-implementable\" (PostgreSQL, S3, **and the memory/remote/DHT byte-store implementations**) vs. \"Jing's own built-in\" (file) — misclassifies `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.remote`, and `dao.jing.dht`. Those are exactly as much Jing's own in-repo code as `dao.jing.file` is (same `dao.jing.*` namespace family, same source tree); PostgreSQL and S3 are the only backends that are actually third-party/not-yet-built. Grouping memory/remote/DHT with PostgreSQL/S3 to justify singling out file is not an accurate use of \"third-party.\"\ncollab/dao-jing/1789700000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-final.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:27:- The new Objective text (`:24-40`) grounds the exception in re-ingestion-across-process-death rather than \"built-in vs. third-party,\" and explicitly corrects the misclassification I raised in r2 (memory/remote/DHT are equally in-repo, not equally \"third-party\" to unbuilt PostgreSQL/S3). This is a strictly better argument than what r1/r2 converged on.\ncollab/dao-jing/1789700000000-architect-review-dao-jing-cbor-final.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:28:- It also resolves my own r2 residual note: `## Backend changes` (`:334-337`) now opens with an explicit pointer back to the Objective's carve-out before its `### Memory and files` subsection, so a reader hitting that section first no longer has to reconstruct the exception from memory. The bare `backend: memory | file | PostgreSQL | S3 | remote | DHT | ...` diagram (`:46`) is still undifferentiated, but it now sits *below* an Objective paragraph that already explains the exception in detail — a minor residual, not a contradiction.\ncollab/dao-jing/storage-space-query-v2.glm-5.3.findings.md:59:(ownership), S (snapshot), E (evaluator), L (lazy published). R5 pins S1-S3\ncollab/dao-jing/storage-space-query-v2.glm-5.3.findings.md:146:this path (the old R5 throw tests are replaced by S1-S3 by the plan\ncollab/dao-jing/1789569973938-review-jing-cbor-design.glm-5.3.findings.md:35:- **Storage correctness (item 5) — carried over and strengthened.** Snapshot copying, CAS insert-if-absent, `:present` byte read-back, fsync-before-ack, torn-tail recovery all descend from the current verified backends; verification improves (byte hash vs re-print). Old-store rejection is achievable: complete old EDN frames never look torn (framing is shared), and EDN text fails CBOR frame decode loudly before any truncation — the required nondestructive-rejection test is already listed. PostgreSQL/S3 reasoning stays at the right altitude.\ncollab/dao-jing/1788977648939-architect-jing-remote-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:255:| S3 | A bind failure throws from `serve-content!` and retains nothing | `[D]` **new** — `serving/start!` answers `transport-error` |\ncollab/dao-jing/1788977648939-architect-jing-remote-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:899:   server on the first's port throws (S3); `(:stop! server)` twice returns\ncollab/dao-jing/1788977648939-architect-jing-remote-v2-plan.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:1017:plus §5.2 #3–#9. They pin N1–N3, N6, N7, N10, N11, S2, S3, S5 through\ntest/dao/stream/remote_channel_test.cljc:655:;; S3b: writer-surface tables, identities dials, detach, drain\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/class_prelude_test.cljc:203:;; ------------------------------------------------------------------- S3\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:2:  \"Stage 0 (the T2 remainder), steps S3 and S4: constructibility by `:kind`\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:114:;; ------------------------------------------------------------------ S3\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:452:           (let [r (S3 {:js/str \"defineProperty\"} o {:js/str \"a\"} d)\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:466:                   (S3 {:js/str \"defineProperty\"} o {:js/str \"a\"} d)\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/object_parity_test.cljc:468:                   (S3 {:js/str \"defineProperty\"} o {:js/str \"a\"} d))))\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs_p6.cljc:30:   arm), S3 (the dynamic-scope profile), S4 (namespaces, `use`, the\ntest/yang/php/antlr/programs_p6.cljc:170:  \"Includes inside functions (P6 S3): the including scope is the function's\ntest/yin/repl/dht_head_test.cljc:674:;; The channel owns the dial's liveness (H2 sign-off notes 1 to 3, S3a-2)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_board_test.cljc:4:   S3a-2).\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_board_test.cljc:623:;; Explicit stop and stream-side liveness (S3a-2)\ntest/yin/vm/linker/head_follow_test.cljc:1087:;; :polled and :answered (S3a-2)\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:258:   S3's tracing (the trace cell and its hooks in the prelude) moves it\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:259:   once more. S3's shared-call prelude revision moves it with `py`.\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:244:   S3 shared-call publication moved the Python module manifest.\"\ntest/yin/vm/semantic/frame_test.cljc:273:              [\"S3\" :window-representation (assoc worked-frame :yin.k/window {-1 nil}) worked-code :wait task]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:300:  \"What the guest sees of each module outcome (S3a, `py/int-result`)\ntest/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:2:  \"yin.vm.linker M4 slice S3 (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md sections 7.2 to\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_fixtures.cljc:43:   bits), and `small`, as the S3a limit tests run.\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:784:  (testing \"S3 arithmetic, power and shifts promote exactly on every host\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:1129:;; C3 slice S3a: integer limit reasons are guest exceptions\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc:2:  \"C3 S3-A/B/C exact operators and range/index audit on all four VMs.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,205p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree/cursor.cljc; sed -n '70,101p' test/dao/data/btree_test.cljc; sed -n '870,891p' docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md; sed -n '74,122p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc; sed -n '270,312p' src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc; rg -n 'max-message|bound|limit' src/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,205p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree/cursor.cljc; sed -n '70,101p' test/dao/data/btree_test.cljc; sed -n '870,891p' docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md; sed -n '74,122p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc; sed -n '270,312p' src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc; rg -n 'max-message|bound|limit' src/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc | head -45\"","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.data.btree.cursor\n  \"A pure, stepped, ascending cursor over PERSISTED B-tree node blobs\n   (docs/design Phase 4b seam; the seam spec r7 as amended by r8).\n\n   The cursor never reads. It is an immutable value that a driver advances:\n\n     (start descriptor comparator)  -> cursor      no I/O; PINS the comparator\n     (step cursor)                  -> outcome     one local transition\n     (supply cursor response)       -> cursor      hand over the requested node\n\n   Outcomes of `step` (exactly one):\n\n     {:status :continue  :cursor c}                local progress, step again\n     {:status :need-node :address a :cursor c}     fetch ONE node, then `supply`\n     {:status :item      :value k :cursor c}       one stored key, in order\n     {:status :done      :cursor c}                terminal\n\n   The descriptor is {:root address-or-nil :count n :branching-factor b\n   :lower nil-or-{:key k :inclusive? bool} :upper nil-or-{:key k\n   :inclusive? bool}}. A nil bound is unbounded; {:key nil ...} is a genuine\n   nil key bound. `start` does not retain the branching factor at all (it is\n   accepted in the descriptor and ignored), and this namespace never rejects\n   a node for occupancy, fill or key count (those are builder policy, not\n   persisted-format rules).\n\n   The persisted shapes are those of `dao.data.btree/node->blob`:\n   a leaf is {:keys [...]}; a branch is {:level n :keys [...] :addresses\n   [...]} whose keys are CHILD MAXIMA. A response is {:address a :node blob}.\n\n   ---- Protocol -------------------------------------------------------------\n\n   One request is pending at a time. Stepping a waiting cursor returns the\n   same request and makes no progress (idempotent). `supply` throws a\n   structured error (ex-data :dao.data.btree.cursor/error) when: no node is\n   pending (:unexpected-response); the address differs from the pending one\n   (:address-mismatch); or the request was already satisfied\n   (:already-satisfied). Only the MOST RECENT satisfied address is remembered,\n   so a stale response naming an earlier satisfied address, when nothing is\n   pending, is classified :unexpected-response rather than :already-satisfied;\n   every such path rejects, so the distinction is diagnostic only. A supplied node is validated by later `step`\n   transitions, never inside `supply`; a malformed node is a structured\n   failure (:malformed-node, :inconsistent-level, :unordered-keys,\n   :separator-mismatch), never a silent empty or an endless traversal.\n\n   ---- Transitions (no multi-node recursive descent, no hidden loop) -------\n\n   begin -> request the root (an empty tree is :done with ZERO requests);\n   validate (chunked) -> enter; enter a branch: choose a child (seek by child\n   maxima, else leftmost), push an immutable path frame, request ONE child;\n   enter a leaf: position (seek) and yield keys one at a time; an exhausted\n   leaf ascends: each popped frame is one :continue, the next sibling is\n   requested, and the descent is again one request per level; the upper bound\n   stops before emitting a key outside it; no bounds is a full walk. Every\n   transition does O(1) work bounded by constants: validation advances at\n   most `validation-chunk` keys per step and a seek is a binary search over a\n   node's keys (at most 32 comparisons).\n\n   Validation (the ONLY checks): leaf/branch shape; a branch has a positive\n   integer level, vector keys and addresses of EQUAL length, every address\n   present; keys strictly ordered under the pinned comparator; a child's level\n   is strictly lower than its parent's (leaves are 0); a fetched child's\n   maximum compares EQUAL to the parent separator under the pinned comparator\n   (comparison equality, never content identity); a response matches the\n   pending address. Yielded keys are also checked strictly increasing across\n   leaves. That check is STRICT because of a safety premise: persisted trees\n   are built from sorted sets, which cannot hold comparator-equal elements, so\n   two comparator-equal consecutive keys are unrepresentable in this format\n   (kind-tied values survive only through differing later comparator fields).\n   A future editor must neither weaken the check to non-strict nor extend it\n   to a format, such as multiset blobs, where comparator-equal neighbors are\n   legal and it would falsely reject. Nothing else: roots, single-child spines and partial final chunks\n   read fine. Validation is incremental and never fetches a sibling to\n   validate the selected path; it does not certify the whole tree.\n\n   Path frames hold the immutable branch blob, its address, level, and the\n   selected child position (which is also the advancement position). No frame\n   holds a storage handle, execution state or clock; cursor state is a local\n   value and is not promised to be serializable. This namespace calls nothing\n   that restores implicitly (no resident-root, node-child, slice, seq on a\n   set) and acquires no dependency on datoms, DaoSpace or any comparator.\n\n   ---- Comparator, sentinels and the stopping rule (r8) ---------------------\n\n   Bounds and node ordering use the comparator pinned at `start`; content\n   equality must never terminate a comparator-equal range. Prefix scans are a\n   caller policy, not inferred by the cursor: the caller seeks with a lower\n   bound, takes keys while its own prefix test (for DaoSpace, `same-slot?`,\n   a comparator tie on the scanned slot) holds, and stops requesting work at\n   the first key outside the prefix. The scan must CONTINUE across values\n   that compare equal but differ in content identity (1 and 1.0, 1.0M and\n   1.00M, 0.0 and -0.0, which interleave by the later comparator fields);\n   content-strict filtering happens AFTER candidate production. Never\n   manufacture a generic upper key by replacing unspecified fields with nil:\n   under nil-first ordering that is an incorrect upper boundary for a prefix\n   range.\n\n   Seek keys are canonical datom slot positions [e a v t m], never\n   physically reordered to match a comparator's field priority. Under the\n   nil-first comparison of sentinel fields:\n\n     | Selected access path               | Lower sentinel       |\n     |------------------------------------|----------------------|\n     | EAVT, entity e                     | [e nil nil nil nil]  |\n     | AEVT, attribute a                  | [nil a nil nil nil]  |\n     | AVET, attribute a and value v      | [nil a v nil nil]    |\n     | VAET, value v                      | [nil nil v nil nil]  |\n     | Full EAVT walk                     | no lower bound       |\n\n   These are seek keys, not stored datoms. (This is usage documentation only:\n   the namespace depends on none of it.)\")\n\n\n(def validation-chunk\n  \"The most keys one validating transition examines.\"\n  32)\n\n\n(defn- fail\n  [kind message data]\n  (throw (ex-info message (assoc data ::error kind))))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; start\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(defn start\n  \"A new cursor over the tree described by `descriptor`, pinning\n   `comparator` (a two-argument function returning a negative, zero or\n   positive number) for its whole life. Performs no I/O.\"\n  [descriptor comparator]\n  (let [{:keys [root lower upper], cnt :count} descriptor]\n    (when-not (fn? comparator)\n      (fail :invalid-descriptor \"a comparator function is required\" {}))\n    (when-not (and (integer? cnt) (not (neg? cnt)))\n      (fail :invalid-descriptor \":count must be a nonnegative integer\" {}))\n    (when (and (pos? cnt) (nil? root))\n      (fail :invalid-descriptor \"a non-empty tree needs a root address\"\n            {:count cnt}))\n    (doseq [[k b] [[:lower lower] [:upper upper]]]\n      (when-not (or (nil? b) (and (map? b) (contains? b :key)))\n        (fail :invalid-descriptor \"a bound is nil or {:key k :inclusive? bool}\"\n              {:bound k})))\n    {::phase (if (or (zero? cnt) (nil? root)) :done :begin)\n     ::cmp comparator\n     ::root root\n     ::lower lower\n     ::upper upper\n     ::seeking? (some? lower)\n     ::path []\n     ::leaf nil\n     ::pending nil\n     ::incoming nil\n     ::vi 0\n     ::shaped? false\n     ::last-key nil\n     ::has-last? false\n     ::satisfied nil}))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; supply\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(defn supply\n  \"Hand the requested node to a waiting cursor. Returns a cursor that the\n   next `step` calls validate. Throws a structured error when nothing is\n   pending, the address differs, or the request was already satisfied.\"\n  [cursor response]\n  (let [pending (::pending cursor)\n        address (:address response)]\n    (cond\n      (not= :awaiting (::phase cursor))\n      (if (and (some? (::satisfied cursor)) (= address (::satisfied cursor)))\n        (fail :already-satisfied \"the request was already satisfied\"\n              {:address address})\n        (fail :unexpected-response \"no node is pending\" {:address address}))\n\n      (not= address (:address pending))\n      (fail :address-mismatch \"response does not match the pending address\"\n            {:expected (:address pending), :actual address})\n\n      :else\n      (assoc cursor\n             ::phase :validate\n             ::pending nil\n             ::incoming {:address address, :blob (:node response)}\n             ::vi 0\n             ::shaped? false\n             ::satisfied address))))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; helpers\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(defn- first-ge\n  \"First index in keys whose key compares >= k; (count keys) when none.\n   A binary search: at most 32 comparisons.\"\n  [cmp ks k]\n  (loop [low 0\n         high (count ks)]\n    (if (< low high)\n      (let [mid (quot (+ low high) 2)]\n        (if (neg? (cmp (nth ks mid) k))\n                 (dec i)))\n        v))))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Invariant walker\n\n(defn- check-node\n  \"Validates the subtree rooted at node; returns {:count n :depth d}.\"\n  [node cmp bf root?]\n  (let [min-fill (quot bf 2)\n        ks (bt/node-keys-vec node)\n        len (count ks)]\n    (is (<= len bf) \"node exceeds branching factor\")\n    (is (every? #(neg? (cmp (ks %) (ks (inc %)))) (range (dec len)))\n        \"keys not strictly ascending within node\")\n    (if (bt/branch? node)\n      (do (is (>= len (if root? 2 min-fill)) \"branch underfilled\")\n          (is (every? #(zero? (cmp (ks %)\n                                   (bt/node-lim-key (bt/node-child node %))))\n                      (range len))\n              \"branch key does not equal child's max key\")\n          (let [subs (mapv #(check-node (bt/node-child node %) cmp bf false)\n                           (range len))]\n            (is (apply = (map :depth subs)) \"leaves at differing depths\")\n            {:count (reduce + (map :count subs)),\n             :depth (inc (:depth (first subs)))}))\n      (do (when-not root? (is (>= len min-fill) \"leaf underfilled\"))\n          {:count len, :depth 0}))))\n\n\n(defn- check-set\n<code>127.0.0.1</code>\n</td>\n<td>\nMust match the composed socket's bind family.\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\nS3 measured a real `dao.space.index/publish-index!` build at the default\nbranching factor of 512 over 512 distinct datoms. It emitted two covered\nindex node blobs and one manifest; the largest canonical value was 26,497\nbytes, and its maximal-cookie `:store` wire value was 26,720 bytes. The\n65,536-byte default therefore covers this measured build. Larger application\nvalues still require an explicit composition limit or refusal.\n\nEvery stream the DHT is composed over is an evicting ring: requests,\nanswers, facts, raw traffic. None is a complete-history log.\n\n## 10. Today's code, and the slices that replace it\n\nWhat the tree holds on 2026-09-30, all of it replaced by this epic with no\ncompatibility path:\n\n\n(defn sha256\n  \"SHA-256 hex digest of the UTF-8 bytes of string s. Rejection on\n   unpaired surrogates via dao.host.text/utf8-encode.\"\n  [s]\n  (bytes->hex (hash/sha256-bytes (text/utf8-encode s))))\n\n\n(defn sha256-bytes\n  \"SHA-256 hex digest of host bytes bs (byte[] on the JVM, Uint8Array on\n   ClojureScript, Uint8List on Dart). `(sha256-bytes (canonical-bytes v))`\n   is the digest for `(content-hash v {:algorithm :sha256})`.\"\n  [bs]\n  (bytes->hex (hash/sha256-bytes bs)))\n\n\n(def default-hash-algorithm\n  \"The default algorithm used for implicit minting (content-hash, segment-key,\n   materialize!). Never consulted when verifying an existing address.\"\n  :blake3)\n\n\n(def registry\n  \"The closed, immutable algorithm registry. Both initial entries produce\n   32-byte (64 lowercase hex character) digests.\"\n  {:blake3 {:address-id \"blake3\", :digest-bytes 32}\n   :sha256 {:address-id \"sha256\", :digest-bytes 32}})\n\n\n(def ^:private registry-by-address-id\n  (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(:address-id v) k])) registry))\n\n\n(def ^:private algorithm-id-pattern\n  \"Algorithm address identifiers are lowercase ASCII alphanumeric strings.\"\n  #\"[a-z0-9]+\")\n\n\n(def ^:private hex-digit-pattern\n  #\"[0-9a-f]+\")\n\n\n(defn blake3-bytes\n  \"BLAKE3 lowercase hex digest of host bytes bs (byte[] on the JVM,\n   Uint8Array on ClojureScript, Uint8List on Dart).\"\n  [bs]\n  (bytes->hex (hash/blake3-bytes bs)))\n\n    [_]\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n     :dao.stream/descriptor the-descriptor\n     :dao.stream/identity (:dao.stream/identity the-descriptor)})\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n\n  (append!\n    [_ v]\n    (if @closed\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}\n      (let [checked (check-outbound v max-bytes)]\n        (if-some [reason (:refusal checked)]\n          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-value\n           :dao.stream.datagram/reason reason}\n          (let [destination (:dao.stream.datagram/destination v)\n                r (try\n                    (send-fn (:dao.stream.datagram/host destination)\n                             (:dao.stream.datagram/port destination)\n                             (:bytes checked))\n                    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\n                      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n                       :dao.stream.datagram/reason (str e)}))]\n            (if (and (map? r) (contains? r :dao.stream/outcome))\n              (if (#{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/transport-error\n                     :dao.stream/closed} (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n                r\n                {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error})\n              {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))))))\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\n\n  (close!\n    [_]\n    (vreset! closed true)\n    (try (close-fn)\n         (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n           nil))\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))\n\n\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:2:  \"In-memory, unbounded, append-only DaoStream v2 transport: the\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:13:   (no capacity is declared; the transport is logically unbounded and\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:156:    ;; after it.  Unbounded means conj is the whole write path — no\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:2:  \"The explicit Jing/transport boundary adapter (D7's condition, U10).\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:18:   byte string that crossed the wrong lane fails loudly at the boundary\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:72:   Transit unwrap is a payload that crossed the wrong boundary.\"\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:95:   boundary operation itself. There is no unwrap-payload: the canonical\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
